LEGACY BANK DES-ECB PROJECT — CONDITIONAL PADDING
Python 3.8+, no external cryptographic libraries.

Files: des.py, client.py, server.py, test_des.py, test_client_server.py

RUN TESTS (with the server stopped):
  python -m py_compile des.py client.py server.py test_des.py test_client_server.py
  python test_des.py
  python test_client_server.py

RUN MANUALLY:
  Terminal 1: python server.py
  Terminal 2: python client.py

Enter TRANSFER to encrypt one 8-byte block with no padding.
Enter PAY:2500.50 to add five padding bytes, making two blocks.
Enter TRANSFERTRANSFER to encrypt two identical blocks with no padding;
the two ciphertext blocks match for the ECB demonstration.

The DES key is hardcoded as BANKKEY! in client.py and server.py.
The DES algorithm uses the same standard tables and 16 Feistel rounds as
Appendix A, without importing cryptographic libraries.

Padding is conditional, NOT standard PKCS#7: only messages whose byte length
is not divisible by 8 receive padding bytes. Each padding byte equals the
number of added bytes, from 1 through 7. Fully aligned messages receive none.

For accurate unpadding, the client transmits an 8-byte header:
  4 bytes: ciphertext length in bytes
  4 bytes: original plaintext length in bytes
The server validates both lengths and any padding, then decrypts and prints
only the original plaintext. Unlike the previous version, the client and
server in this ZIP must be used together because the header format changed.

The client accepts 1–4096 ASCII bytes, including digits and punctuation.
The server has a five-second deadline to receive each complete transaction.
The maximum plaintext length is 4096 bytes. Local socket: 127.0.0.1:5000.
The same static 64-bit key is used for encryption and decryption; the 8 parity
bit positions are omitted by DES Permuted Choice 1, giving 56 effective bits.

For a classroom demonstration only: DES-ECB, a hardcoded key, and an
unauthenticated message length are unsuitable for real banking security.
